d Body Applications","url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s326571/11-appendix-2-approved-admitted-body-applications.pdf","committee":"Pensions Board","date":"14/07/2026","key":false}],"read_documents":[],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-29","committee":"Adults Scrutiny Panel","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-30","committee":"Pensions Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-30","committee":"Economy and Growth Scrutiny Panel","time":"6.45 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-01","committee":"Health Scrutiny Panel","time":"1.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-07","committee":"Community Safety, Residents and Environmental Services Scrutiny Panel","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-13","committee":"Pensions Board","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-14","committee":"Cabinet","time":"4.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-20","committee":"Scrutiny Board","time":"4.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Children,Young and People Scrutiny Panel","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Resources and Equality Scrutiny Panel","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-21","committee":"Audit and Risk Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-22","committee":"Scrutiny Board","time":"4.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-23","committee":"Council","time":"5.45 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Wolverhampton repays \u00a317m in loans amid sharp rate hike demands","lead":"Wolverhampton Council rejected proposals to increase rates on three bank loans totaling \u00a317m and chose instead to repay them in full. LOBO loans are bank loans where lenders can propose new terms on set dates. Between July 2025 and January 2026, a \u00a37m Commerzbank loan came with a proposed rate of 6.19%, compared with its existing 3.60%; two \u00a35m Dexia loans proposed 6.21% and 6.26%, against existing rates of 4.26% and 4.29%. According to the Treasury Management Annual Report, this refinancing required \u00a317m in cash but protected the council from substantially higher day-to-day running costs.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"Appendix A Treasury Management Annual Report 2526 Activity Monitoring Quarter One 2627","paper_url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s329075/329075.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Pensions Committee","paper_title":"08. Appendix 1 Quarterly Accounts Q1 2026-27 WMPF","paper_url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s329835/329835.pdf","headline":"Current assets in the pension fund fell 47% in the quarter, from \u00a388.8m to \u00a347.1m, with no explanation provided","detail":"The net assets statement shows current assets declining significantly from \u00a388.8m at 31 March to \u00a347.1m at 30 June, a reduction of \u00a341.7m. The bulk annuity insurance buy-in policy also decreased from \u00a385.3m to \u00a382.2m. The accounts contain no explanation for either movement.","category":"other","matters":"A sharp quarterly decline in liquid current assets may indicate cash flow timing or reflect working capital reductions that the council or a lender would need to understand, particularly if it signals liquidity pressures or changes in fund management","figures":"Current assets: \u00a388.8m to \u00a347.1m (-\u00a341.7m, -47%); bulk annuity policy: \u00a385.3m to \u00a382.2m (-\u00a33.1m)","quote":"88.8 Current Assets 47.1","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Pensions Committee","paper_title":"08. Appendix 1 Quarterly Accounts Q1 2026-27 WMPF","paper_url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s329835/329835.pdf","headline":"Pension fund grew by \u00a31.267bn in Q1 2026-27 with net investment returns of \u00a31.359bn","detail":"The fund's assets increased from \u00a323.368bn at 31 March 2026 to \u00a324.636bn at 30 June 2026, a 5.4% quarterly gain. Investment returns totalled \u00a31.359bn, comprising \u00a31.336bn of positive changes in investment values, \u00a323.2m investment income, and offset by \u00a30.1m of other adjustments. Pooled investment vehicles increased by \u00a31.183bn and overseas equities rose by \u00a3140.2m, while direct property holdings declined by \u00a313.5m.","category":"reserves","matters":"A growing and well-performing pension fund reduces the likelihood of the council needing to increase contributions from its budget to cover shortfalls, strengthening the council's overall financial position and borrowing capacity","figures":"Fund grew from \u00a323.368bn to \u00a324.636bn (increase \u00a31.267bn); net investment return \u00a31.359bn; investment value gains \u00a31.336bn","quote":"Net Increase in the Fund During the Period 1,267.1","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Pensions Committee","paper_title":"10. Appendix 1 Performance and Workload Dashboard","paper_url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s329923/329923.pdf","headline":"SCAPE factor change is halting pension casework and expected to worsen performance in coming months.","detail":"Retirement finalisation cases are currently on hold due to a SCAPE (Superannuation Contributions Adjusted for Past Experience) factor change. The fund explicitly flags this as 'expected to impact performance in the coming months.' SCAPE changes directly drive pension valuations and employer contribution rates. The paper does not quantify the scale of the performance impact or the contribution rate effect.","category":"other","matters":"As an employer in the scheme, Wolverhampton's contributions are set by SCAPE and fund valuations. A change that degrades casework performance may indicate a broader revaluation cycle affecting the council's employer contribution liability and future budget.","figures":"Currently 'a number of cases' on hold; normally over 96% of retirement finalisation cases completed within two weeks","quote":"A number of cases are currently on hold due to the SCAPE factor change, which is expected to impact performance in the coming months.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Pensions Committee","paper_title":"12. Quarterly Investment Report Final","paper_url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s329841/329841.pdf","headline":"The WMTL separate fund is eliminating all equity exposure and moving to 55% liability-driven investment by July 2027, a dramatic de-risking shift from 17% equities and 26% LDI","detail":"The West Midlands Travel Limited (WMTL) admitted body scheme is transitioning from a balanced allocation (17% listed equities, 21% multi-asset credit, 26% LDI) to a purely defensive strategy (0% equities, 0% multi-asset credit, 55% LDI) effective 1 July 2027. This eliminates all growth assets in favour of liabilities hedging. The transition is described as driven by the pace of Private Debt commitment drawdowns, but the completed allocation represents a fundamental shift in investment posture.","category":"other","matters":"Moving to zero equity exposure and maximum liability hedging may indicate either a scheme surplus being locked in or a deficit requiring conservative positioning, either way signalling a material change in the sponsor's or administrator's risk assessment of this former Wolverhampton-related scheme.","figures":"WMTL listed equities moving from 17% current to 0% by July 2027; LDI rising from 26% to 55%; multi-asset credit falling from 21% to 0%; private debt holdings (\u00a349m) increasing as growth assets are withdrawn","quote":"WMTL is in the process of transitioning its allocation towards its final target allocation as commitments to Private Debt are drawn down. Interim targets have been put in place predicated on the expected pace of drawdown.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Pensions Committee","paper_title":"12. Quarterly Investment Report Final","paper_url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s329841/329841.pdf","headline":"Infrastructure assets have experienced write-downs on legacy transport investments due to refinancing challenges","detail":"The Infrastructure portfolio has incurred actual impairments on historical transport sector assets facing refinancing difficulties. The portfolio generated 6.4% returns over one year but has been materially impacted by these write-downs over longer periods, contributing to underperformance of 1.9% per annum against its benchmark over 5 years.","category":"other","matters":"Write-downs represent actual losses on fund investments and signal either poor past investment decision-making or deteriorating conditions in the transport infrastructure sector, both of which raise questions about fund governance and asset quality that lenders use to assess council financial stewardship.","figures":"Infrastructure portfolio 6.4% over 1 year but underperformed by 1.9% per annum over 5 years due to write-downs","quote":"Long-term performance has been subdued by write-downs on legacy transport assets linked to refinancing challenges, as well as the concentrated nature of the portfolio in its early years.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-17"},"name":"Wolverhampton","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-24","since_iso":"2026-08-25","distress":false,"headline":"Three LOBO loans totaling \u00a317m were called in 2025-2026; the council repaid them at par rather than accept new rates of 6.19-6.26%","lead":"LOBO calls materialize the refinancing risk embedded in the council's debt portfolio. The high rates offered (60-173 basis points above existing rates) show market stress on LOBO lending at the time. A lender cares whether the council can refinance when\u2026","also":[],"watch":"Adults Scrutiny Panel on 2026-09-29","n_findings":1,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Appendix A Treasury Management Annual Report 2526 Activity Monitoring Quarter One 2627","top_paper_url":"https://wolverhampton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s329075/329075.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":99,"news_drafts_total":1}}